<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Academic Achievement and Skill Development – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development: How?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a review of a recent event, place, or product. Interactive: Write collaborative stories or articles.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a review of a study app or tool, describing its features and usefulness for academic success (e.g., "This app is great for organizing notes. It helps you stay focused and manage your time better.").
Can collaborate with classmates to write an article on effective study habits, sharing personal experiences and advice (e.g., "Otto suggested that if I studied earlier, I wouldn't feel so rushed before exams. He recommends setting daily study goals to avoid last-minute cramming.").
Can write a short review of a recent school event, highlighting its impact on academic skills (e.g., "The study skills workshop was helpful. We learned how to take better notes and manage time effectively.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various study tools (e.g., a planner, a flashcard app, a textbook, a mind-mapping software). Students are asked to write down one word or short phrase that describes how each tool contributes to academic success or development. For example, for a planner, students might write "organize time." This activates prior vocabulary and sets the theme for writing about useful tools.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes an example sentence on the board, modeling how to give advice or review a tool using target grammar: "If I wanted to improve my time management, I would use a digital planner. It really helps you organize your study goals." The teacher highlights the second conditional structure and the modal verb "helps." The teacher also models the stress pattern for "PROgress" (noun) versus "proGRESS" (verb) if it comes up in student examples.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What type of sentence is 'If I wanted to improve, I would use...'? Is it about a real situation or an imaginary one?" (Expected answer: Imaginary/Hypothetical.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'It helps you organize,' am I giving a command or a suggestion?" (Expected answer: A suggestion/describing a benefit.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides students with two short sample texts: a) a review of a fictional study app (e.g., "FocusFlow") and b) an excerpt from an online article on effective study habits. These texts are designed to be B1.1 level and incorporate the target grammar (second conditional, modals for advice, first conditional) and vocabulary (e.g., <i>achievement, skill, development, advice, study, goals, improvement, focus, organize, recommend, motivate, disciplined</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand how to recognize and analyze the structure and language used in written reviews and advice-giving articles. This helps them later in productive writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read both texts quickly and identify their main purpose (e.g., "Text A reviews an app, Text B gives study tips.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases from the texts to their function. For example: "If you want to achieve better grades..." (advice using first conditional), "You should definitely try this app..." (strong recommendation), "It helps you stay focused..." (describing usefulness).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Spotting:</b> Students underline all instances of second conditional sentences and modals for advice in the texts.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences and short paragraphs using the second conditional for giving advice, modals for advice (should, must, can), and the future simple for first conditional. Key vocabulary includes <i>achievement, skill, development, advice, study, goals, improvement, practice, focus, feedback, organize, recommend, suggest, plan, discipline, motivation, time management.</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Rewriting:</b> Students are given sentences in simple present or imperative forms related to study habits. They must rewrite these sentences using the second conditional or modals for advice.
                        <br><i>Example:</i> "Study every day." → "You should study every day to improve." or "If you studied every day, you would see great improvement."
                        <br><i>Example:</i> "Be organized." → "You must be organized to achieve your goals."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Paragraph Starters:</b> The teacher provides paragraph starters related to reviewing a study tool or giving academic advice. Students complete the paragraphs, ensuring they use at least one second conditional sentence and two modals for advice, along with 3-4 target vocabulary words.
                        <br><i>Example starter for review:</i> "The 'Study Buddy' app is a great tool for students who want to develop better habits. If you were struggling with procrastination, you..."
                        <br><i>Example starter for advice:</i> "Effective time management is a key skill for academic success. You should..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Collaborative Advice Building:</b> In pairs, students choose one common academic challenge (e.g., "difficulty focusing," "poor time management," "lack of motivation"). They then collaboratively write three sentences of advice for this challenge, ensuring each sentence uses a different target grammar structure (second conditional, modal 'should/must', first conditional). They share their sentences with another pair for peer-checking on grammar and vocabulary use.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will engage in a collaborative writing task to produce a short article.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> In groups of three, students will write a short article (approx. 150-200 words) for the school's online newsletter titled "Boosting Your Academic Potential: Tips from Your Peers." The article should address effective study habits and academic skill development. Each group member must contribute at least one paragraph focusing on a specific aspect (e.g., time management, note-taking, motivation, overcoming procrastination), incorporating personal experiences and advice for peers. They *must* use at least two instances of the second conditional for advice, three modals for advice (<i>should, must, can</i>), and one first conditional sentence in their article. They are also encouraged to use a minimum of 8 vocabulary words from the list (e.g., <i>strategy, discipline, focus, progress, achievement, feedback, organize, recommend</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Instructions for students:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Work in your groups of three to draft an article for the school newsletter. Remember to use inclusive language like 'Let's share' and 'You should try...'. Focus on explaining *how* students can achieve academic goals."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Each of you needs to write one section or paragraph, contributing to the overall article. Make sure to integrate our target grammar and vocabulary."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Remember to describe features and usefulness, and provide actionable advice. For example, 'If you organized your notes better, you would save so much study time.'"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups submit their collaboratively written article, "Boosting Your Academic Potential: Tips from Your Peers."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist during the writing process, noting group collaboration, the use of target grammar/vocabulary, and task progress. A simple rubric will be used for evaluating the final written product.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric/Checklist will include):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear communication of advice and insights into academic skill development.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate and appropriate use of second conditional for advice (at least 2 instances).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate and appropriate use of modals for advice (should, must, can) (at least 3 instances).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of at least 8 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>achievement, strategy, motivation, discipline, improve, focus, recommend, organize</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Coherence and organization of the article (introduction, body paragraphs, conclusion).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and equitable contribution from group members (observed).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One specific piece of advice I wrote using a modal verb was: _______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about writing the article, and how did I or my group overcome it?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student self-reflection and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using the second conditional and different modal verbs help me express advice more effectively in my writing?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new vocabulary words did I actively use in my writing today, and how will I continue to practice them for academic improvement?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in facilitating students' writing of advice and reviews? Did they successfully integrate the target grammar and vocabulary for B1.1 level complexity?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) to ensure students can consolidate this written knowledge and apply it to a broader project? Perhaps more structured sentence frames or a bank of linking phrases for longer texts."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5, students will mediate and integrate information from their articles and other sources to create a larger 21st Century Skills Project related to academic achievement. They will summarize and present their findings and advice, potentially using their written articles as a base for a presentation or a peer counseling session.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>